        "---\n",
        "## 3. Split into Training and Testing Sets\n",
        "**Goal:** set aside data the model never sees during training, so I get an honest measure of how well it works on new tracts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Rl9kEYLQWeiE"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (before the split):**\n",
        "I split the data **before** scaling so the test set stays completely unseen. I use an 80/20 split (`test_size=0.2`): with 735 tracts that leaves 588 for training and 147 for testing, which is enough rows to fit a model and still test it. I set `random_state=42` so the split is the same every time I run the notebook. I also use `stratify=state_label`: the state explained a large share of the outcome in the EDA step (DC, MD, and VA have very different average ranks), so stratifying keeps the mix of states the same in both sets and prevents the test set from being, for example, mostly Virginia by chance. I split the weights and tract IDs in the same call so every row stays lined up."
      ]
    },
