        "---\n",
        "## 4. Standardize the Numeric Features\n",
        "**Goal:** put the numeric predictors on a common scale (mean 0, standard deviation 1) using `StandardScaler`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3Ot5MOgWWeiE"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (before the scaling cell):**\n",
        "`StandardScaler` subtracts a column's mean and divides by its standard deviation, so every scaled predictor has mean 0 and standard deviation 1. I fit the scaler on the **training set only** and then apply the same transformation to the test set. If I fit it on all the data, the test rows would help set the means and standard deviations, which leaks information from the test set into training and makes the test score look better than it should. I scale only the continuous numeric predictors. I do **not** scale the `state_MD` and `state_VA` dummies (they are already 0/1 and should stay readable as \"is this state or not\"), the target `y` (I want to predict the real income rank), or the identifiers and weights."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "cRl9tuNvWeiE",
        "outputId": "2dde03a1-1b3f-4198-feff-11197f907792"
      },
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Scaled columns: 15 | Left as they are (dummies): ['state_MD', 'state_VA']\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# Which columns get scaled? The continuous predictors, not the 0/1 dummies\n",
        "scale_cols = numeric_predictors\n",
        "dummy_cols = list(state_dummies.columns)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Fit the scaler on the TRAINING data only, then transform both sets\n",
        "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
        "X_train_scaled = X_train.copy()\n",
        "X_test_scaled = X_test.copy()\n",
        "X_train_scaled[scale_cols] = scaler.fit_transform(X_train[scale_cols])\n",
        "X_test_scaled[scale_cols] = scaler.transform(X_test[scale_cols])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Scaled columns:\", len(scale_cols), \"| Left as they are (dummies):\", dummy_cols)"
      ]
